      "side": 1,
      "message": "The accounting looks right to me: set and clear both take the charge from `mm_is_p3s_4k(mm)`, and 4KB VMAs never get PMD or PUD leaves.\n\nCould the body say what fails without it? As far as I can see, this restores the upstream check that aosp/4312475 relaxed. Since then, `page_table_check_set()` only reports a writable anon mapping once more than `P3S_SLICES_PER_PAGE` PTEs map the page, so a second writable mapping of an anon page in a native process isn\u0027t caught anymore. `P3S_SLICES_PER_PAGE` is 4 on every 16KB kernel, so that also happens without `CONFIG_ARM64_PER_PROCESS_PAGE_SIZE`.\n\nSomething like:\n\n```\npage_table_check_set() allows P3S_SLICES_PER_PAGE anon mappings of a\npage before it reports a writable one, so that a 4KB process can map\nall slices of a page. On 16KB kernels, a second writable mapping of an\nanon page in a native process then goes unnoticed.\n\nCount anon mappings in 4KB slices instead: a native PTE, PMD or PUD\ncharges every slice of the pages it maps, and a PTE of a 4KB process\ncharges one, on both the set and clear paths. Without P3S, each\nmapping charges one, as upstream does.\n```\n\nWould you also mind naming page_table_check in the subject, e.g. `ANDROID: P3S: mm: page_table_check: Restore the anon check for native PTEs`?\n\nNit: as on aosp/4409398, could you wrap the body at 72 columns and drop the blank line between `Bug:` and `Change-Id:`?",
